{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>And the souls that do shall be cut off.</b> We find that the extirpations (karet) in the Torah fall into three categories: there is karet for the body alone, karet for the soul alone, and karet for both together, for body and soul. Karet for the body alone will be in years or in days: for one who is righteous and whose majority is merits, yet stumbles in a transgression that incurs karet, he will die in the middle of his days, in the manner of that student who was with his wife in the days of her purification — he in his garment and she in hers — and he had not stumbled in any other transgression, yet died in the middle of his days, as is found in tractate Shabbat. This is karet of years. But karet of days is, for example, an old man who transgressed a karet obligation: it is no longer possible for there to be karet of years, since he has already reached the days of old age; but there will be karet of days — that he will not complete his appointed days. This is what the Sages, of blessed memory, said in Mo'ed Katan, chapter 'These May Shave,' at the end: Rav Yosef, when he was sixty years old, made a festive day for the Rabbis. He said: 'I have emerged from karet of years.' Abbaye said to him: 'Granted that the Master has emerged from karet of years — from karet of days, has the Master emerged?' He said to him: 'At least take half in your hand.' And because this karet of years is well-known while karet of days is not well-known and not recognized, therefore the Sages, of blessed memory, said that the Holy One, blessed be He, makes it known — that is, the old man dies within three days, and behold, this is a sign that he transgressed a karet obligation. This is what they said in the Jerusalem Talmud: 'An old man who ate forbidden fat or who desecrated the Sabbath — who informs that he is in karet?' And there they answer: 'If he died within one day, this is a death of wrath; within two days, a death of consternation; within three days, a death of karet.' And concerning this karet, which is for the body alone, it is said: ״ונכרת האיש ההוא מקרב עמו״ — that is to say, that his body shall be cut off in years or in days, but his soul will immediately merit, upon separation from the body, life in the world of souls, as well as the resurrection of the dead, as well as the world to come that follows the resurrection. Karet for the soul alone: one who sinned in a grave transgression that incurs karet, such as one who ate leaven on Passover, or ate or performed labor on the Day of Atonement, or sinned in forbidden sexual relations — and whose sins were more numerous than his merits — the punishment of karet reaches his soul after it separates from the body: that it shall be cut off from the life of the world of souls. And concerning this karet it is said: ״ונכרתו הנפשות העושות״, (Leviticus 19:8) ״ונכרתה הנפש ההיא מעמיה״, (ibid. 22) ״ונכרתה הנפש ההיא מלפני״, and likewise (ibid. 23) ״והאבדתי את הנפש ההיא״ — this teaches that karet is nothing but destruction. And it is possible that he has no karet on his body but only on his soul, for sometimes he lives long days and tranquil days, in the manner of what is written (Ecclesiastes 7:15): ״ויש רשע מאריך ברעתו״. Karet for both together, for body and soul: in grave karet such as one who worships idolatry, concerning whom it is written (Numbers 15) ״את YHWH הוא מגדף״ — and we find no double karet in the Torah except in his case, for his body and soul are both cut off: he is banished from this world and from the world to come. And concerning this karet it is said (ibid.): ״הכרת תכרת הנפש ההיא עונה בה״, and the Sages, of blessed memory, expounded: ״הכרת״ — in this world; ״תכרת״ — for the world to come. According to this understanding, in karet of the soul alone, the soul liable to karet — whether in lighter karet or in grave karet — is utterly annihilated from its proper existence, since karet is destruction. If so, the meaning of karet is that the soul is cut off from eternal life and utterly annihilated, and this is: ״ונכרתה הנפש ההיא מעמיה״, and the meaning of 'from among its people' is that it is cut off from the vitality of the other souls that are 'its people,' for they are alive, and this one will not live at all but will be utterly annihilated — ״ולא ישא Elohim נפשו״. This is the very expression it uses (Leviticus 22:3): ״ונכרתה הנפש ההיא מלפני״ — for He, blessed be He, is in every place, and this one is utterly annihilated from every place. This is the opinion of the sage Rabbi Abraham, of blessed memory." "<b>Also</b> the Rambam, of blessed memory, is of this opinion, as evidenced by what he wrote in chapter 8 of Hilkhot Teshuvah: 'The reward of the righteous is that they will merit the pleasantness of the life of the world to come and live in goodness, and the punishment of the wicked is that they will not merit this life but will be cut off and die. And whoever does not merit this life — that is the dead one who does not live forever, but is cut off in his wickedness and destroyed like a beast. And this is the karet of which it is written (Numbers 31): ״הכרת תכרת״. From the received tradition they expounded: ״הכרת״ — in this world; ״תכרת״ — for the life of the world to come — that is, that the soul which departed from the body in this world does not merit the life of the world to come, but is cut off even from the world to come' — end of the Rav's language." "<b>And one may</b> wonder at this, for the absolute wicked man who dies without repentance — Gehenna was created only for him. And just as the absolute righteous man's soul delights in eternal delight, so the absolute wicked man deserves that his soul be punished with eternal punishment. If it is utterly annihilated, as these sages hold, where is the punishment, and peace? And if you say that Gehenna was created for the intermediate, then it would be better for the absolute wicked than for the intermediate — and this is something reason cannot accept. Moreover, if the law were so, how would these sages not enumerate these sins — whose souls are liable to karet and utterly annihilated — together with those of whom we learned in the Mishnah that they have no share in the world to come? For the meaning of 'they have no share' is that they have no distinct portion of their own, but they benefit and are sustained from various treasure-houses of righteousness stored away for those who did not earn it, like a pauper who has nothing to eat and depends on his fellow's table. And even those absolute sinners who descend and do not ascend have rest on Sabbaths and festivals. Therefore one must explain that the rational soul cannot be annihilated at all. And the explanation of ״ונכרתה הנפש ההיא מעמיה״ is: from the place from which it was hewn, from there it is cut off. Furthermore, how can we decree the total annihilation of the soul, since they said: 'They are judged there for generations upon generations' — behold, the intention is not in their annihilation but in their continued existence, in eternal punishment for generations. And surely there are no wicked men who deny YHWH and Moses His servant more than Korah and his congregation, who were destroyed from this world and the world to come; nonetheless they were not annihilated, for they live in Gehenna, enduring in their punishment. And in the future they will merit the resurrection of the dead, as it is said (I Samuel 2:6): ״מוריד שאול ויעל״. And the meaning of 'from among its people' — from the rest of the souls that are its people — is that it shall be cut off from that place and not return there. And likewise the expression ״ונכרתה״ indicates, like a branch cut off from the tree from which it would have lived; but his soul shall enjoy the radiance of the Shekhinah, though not in its place, since it accepted its sentence that it was cut off from its place from which it was hewn. This is the meaning of 'from among its people' — that is, from the root of its origin and from its species, and it is the place from which it was hewn. This is the opinion of the Ramban, of blessed memory. And likewise when it says ״ונכרתה הנפש ההיא מלפני״ — the meaning is: from the place where His Shekhinah dwells, and that is the Land of Israel, for there is the gate of heaven and from there the souls ascend. Therefore the devout long to die there. And it is like the matter said of Jonah son of Amittai the prophet, peace be upon him (Jonah 1:10): ״כי מלפני YHWH הוא בורח״ — whose meaning is: from the place where His Shekhinah dwells continually, and this is the Land of Israel. For he was fleeing from the Land of Israel to outside the Land in order to distance himself from prophecy, since prophecy is not revealed to a prophet in full except in the Land of Israel. And likewise when it says concerning one defiled by a corpse (Numbers 19:20): ״ואיש אשר יטמא ולא יתחטא ונכרתה הנפש ההיא מתוך הקהל״ — those who conduct themselves in purity — and this one separated from them, therefore his soul is cut off from its place and will not rest in its company; but surely it will find rest, though not in its place, after it has accepted its sentence in karet that it is cut off from its place. And it shall not be like the soul of a beast, which is annihilated, Heaven forbid." "<b>And behold,</b> from the wisdom of Onkelos, of blessed memory, you will not find that he translates anywhere in the entire Torah: ״ונכרתה הנפש ההיא״ as anything but 'וישתצי אנשא ההוא' (and that man shall be destroyed). And that he attaches the matter to the person of the man rather than to the soul is evidence that he holds that the soul is not cut off and utterly annihilated; rather, it is cut off from the place from which it was hewn, and it benefits and delights in another place — but not with the measure of that delight to which it would have been entitled in its first place, had it merited it." "<b>But as for</b> the philosophers, they hold regarding the human soul that it is merely a disposition (hekhinah) for receiving the study of wisdom and Torah within the human body. And they bring a clear proof that a person cannot deny: for from the beginning of formation, when the soul enters the body, it has no knowledge or understanding; and since it gradually grows wiser and its wisdom increases all the while that the body grows — this indicates that the soul is nothing but a disposition. For if the soul were wise at the time of its entry into the body, it would necessarily follow that the body would be wise upon emerging from its mother's womb." "<b>Know that</b> the opinions of the philosophers are not a principle for us, for they are only from the side of inquiry, and they did not know and did not understand — in darkness they walk. But the discerning of Israel have nothing but the words of our Rabbis, of blessed memory, the sages of truth, who received the truth from the mouths of the prophets, and they are the ones who knew all matters in their truth. For the soul in its perfection and wisdom enters into the body at the beginning of formation, and the proof of this is (Genesis 2:7): ״ויפח באפיו נשמת חיים״ — go out and learn who is the one who breathes. If so, the soul is eternal and enduring, supremely elevated in the utmost elevation, and because of the exceeding greatness of its elevation, creation is not mentioned regarding it as regarding the rest of the work of Genesis, not even in allusion. And this wisdom that belongs to the soul while it is still in its mother's womb is forgotten from it when it is born and comes forth into the air of the world. And the sages of truth likened this wisdom in their Midrash to a lit lamp that is extinguished when it comes forth into the air of the world. And this is what they said in the Gemara at the end of the chapter HaMapelet: a lit lamp is placed upon his head, and he gazes and sees from one end of the world to the other. And do not wonder, for behold, a person sleeps here and sees a dream in Spain, as it is said (Job 29:3): ״בהלו נרו עלי ראשי״. When he comes forth into the air of the world, an angel comes and slaps him on his mouth and causes him to forget the entire Torah, as it is said (Genesis 4:7): ״לפתח חטאת רובץ״." "<b>And likewise</b> they said further in the Midrash: they place a lit lamp upon his head and he gazes in it from one end of the world to the other, as it is said: ״בהלו נרו עלי ראשי לאורו אלך חשך״. In the morning the angel takes him and brings him to the Garden of Eden and shows him the righteous who sit with their crowns upon their heads. And the angel says to him: know who these are that you see — these were formed like you within their mother's womb and came forth into the world and kept the commandments of the Holy One, blessed be He, and therefore they merited and attained this goodness. And you are destined to come forth into the world, and if you merit and keep the Torah you will merit to sit in their company, and if not, you will sit in another place. In the evening he brings him to Gehenna and shows him the wicked, who are tormented by the angels of destruction with rods of fire, and they cry out, woe, woe. And the angel says to him: know — these who are being burned were formed like you and came forth into the world and did not keep the commandments of the Holy One, blessed be He, and therefore they were punished and came to this disgrace. And you are destined to come forth into the world; be righteous and do not be wicked, so that you may live. And from where do we know this is so? For it is said (Proverbs 4:4): ״ויורני ויאמר לי יתמך דברי לבך שמור מצותי וחיה״. When his time arrives to come forth into the air of the world, the angel immediately strikes him and extinguishes his lamp and brings him out against his will, and he forgets all that he saw, and brings him out into the air of the world. And because of this the infant cries as soon as he comes forth into the air of the world — end of quotation. And they explained to us through this that the soul in its wisdom and perfection comes into the body, but the angel causes it to forget immediately upon birth, by decree of the Holy One, blessed be He, so that he may strive and labor in learning and receive reward." "<b>And</b> the Ramban, of blessed memory, wrote that the Torah's mention of karet (excision) of the sinning soul is the testimony and proof of the endurance of souls that did not sin — for it is not the way of the upright King to punish His servant for transgressing His commandment yet not reward him with goodness when he fulfills it. And certainly, through the punishment of karet explicitly stated for the sinning soul, the reward and recompense for the righteous soul will be clarified." "<b>And behold,</b> the soul of man is נר ה׳ — a lamp of HaShem — breathed into his nostrils from the mouth of the Most High, the soul of Shaddai, as it is said (Genesis 2:7): ״ויפח באפיו נשמת חיים״. And behold, it is in its nature not to die, and it is not composite such that its composition could be dissolved and it would have a cause of coming-to-be and passing-away like composite things; rather, its endurance is intrinsic and stands forever like the separate intellects. Therefore He adds explanation regarding the matter of karet and says: ״עונה בה״ — its iniquity is in it, that is, the iniquity that is in it is what will cut it off. And therefore Scripture does not need to say that through the merit of commandments its endurance will be, for this is self-evident; rather, it says that through the punishment of transgressions it becomes defiled and impure and is cut off from its proper endurance. And this is what our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: מקרב עמה — it draws near with it; ועמה שלום — and with it is peace. For the karet of the sinning soul indicates the endurance of all other souls that did not sin, and they are עמה — with it — and they are at peace. And this is why the Torah warns regarding the matter of karet, which is a wondrous matter, and does not promise endurance, which is proper and natural — for every single thing returns to its foundation and to the primal source of its emergence and its first cause: the elements return to their cause, and the dust to its origin, and the soul to its root and foundation, which is the active intellect, in the manner of what is written (Ecclesiastes 12:7): ״וישוב העפר על הארץ כשהיה והרוח תשוב אל האלהים אשר נתנה״."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {8 "At the end of comment 3, 'הבינה' is rendered literally as 'the active intellect' following the philosophical register R. Bahya invokes; the Hebrew could also read simply 'the understanding.' The phrase מקרב עמה ועמה שלום appears to be a rabbinic dictum whose precise source is not cited here."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:27:27.136943630Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}